Lecture 3 — Backend & REST API
Goal of this lecture: understand why an LLM call belongs behind your own server, design a small REST API for it, model the request and response with Pydantic, and add error handling and a simple rate limit. By the end you should be able to expose a chat completion as a POST /api/chat endpoint with typed request/response schemas.
In Lectures 1–2 you called the model directly from a Python script and learned to get structured JSON back. That is perfect for experimenting, but it is not how a real product works. A web or mobile app cannot ship your API key to every user's browser, and you want a place to validate input, enforce limits, and log what happens. That place is your backend.

1. Why put the LLM behind your own backend?
When the browser calls the model provider directly, the provider's API key has to live in the client — where anyone can open dev-tools and steal it. They could then spend your money or impersonate your app. The fix is a server you control sitting in the middle: the browser talks to your API, and only your API talks to the model.

Once you own that middle layer, you get a lot more than secrecy:

Hide the key. The provider key stays in a server-side environment variable and never crosses the network to the client.
Validate. Reject empty, oversized, or malformed requests before you spend tokens on them.
Rate-limit. Cap how often each user can call the model, so one person (or a bug) can't run up your bill or starve everyone else.
Log & observe. Record requests and responses for debugging, evaluation (Lecture 8), and abuse detection.
Inject context. Add the system prompt, retrieved documents (Lecture 6), or tool definitions (Lecture 10) on the server, so the client only sends the user's message.
Swap models freely. Change gemma-4-E4B-it for something else, or route to a different provider, without shipping a new frontend.
The course's reference app — a small FastAPI backend deployed at <https://rag-demo.nat-d.uk> (source) — is exactly such a backend: a real server that exposes a POST /api/chat endpoint and forwards chat calls to a model, with the provider key held server-side. We'll use it as a running example. Concretely, "hide the key" is real there: the key lives only in a server-side environment variable, and the browser never sees it — it just POSTs a message to /api/chat and gets a reply back. So from openai import OpenAI; OpenAI() reads its key from the server's environment, without that key ever appearing in client code or the page. The server holds it; the client only ever sees its effect.

What a "server" actually is
Until now your code ran and exited. A backend is different: a long-running process that waits, and for every incoming HTTP request sends back exactly one HTTP response. A request is just text — a method and path, some headers, and an optional JSON body:

POST /api/chat HTTP/1.1
Host: courses.nat-d.uk
Content-Type: application/json

{"message": "Hi", "temperature": 0.7}
and the response is text too — a status line, headers, and a body:

HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: application/json

{"reply": "Hello!", "model": "gemma-4-E4B-it"}
Everything in this lecture is about shaping those two messages: which verb and path you accept, what JSON body you require, and which status code you answer with. FastAPI hides the raw text, but it's worth seeing once — the browser in Lecture 4 sends exactly this.

A note on CORS: when a frontend is served from a different origin than the API, the browser blocks the call unless the API explicitly allows that origin. This app dodges it entirely — FastAPI serves the built SPA and the API from the same origin (and Vite proxies /api in dev), so there's nothing to configure. You'd add CORS only if you hosted the frontend separately.
2. REST in 90 seconds
A REST API exposes resources (things) at URLs, and uses the HTTP verb to say what to do with one. The verbs you'll use most:

Verb	Meaning	Side effects?
GET	Read a resource	No — safe to repeat/cache
POST	Create something, or run an action	Yes
PUT / PATCH	Replace / partially update	Yes
DELETE	Remove a resource	Yes
A chat completion does work — it spends tokens and produces a new reply — so it is a POST, not a GET. As a rule, anything with a side effect or a request body is a POST.

The server answers with a status code that tells the client what happened:

2xx — success. 200 OK (here's your data), 201 Created.
4xx — the client's fault. 400 Bad Request, 401 Unauthorized, 404 Not Found, 422 Unprocessable Entity (well-formed JSON but invalid fields), 429 Too Many Requests.
5xx — the server's fault. 500 Internal Server Error, 502 Bad Gateway, 504 Gateway Timeout (an upstream like the model failed).
Picking honest status codes is half of good API design: the client can branch on them ("show a 'slow down' toast on 429, a validation error on 422").

3. Modelling requests and responses with Pydantic
Your API is a contract. The cleanest way to write that contract in FastAPI is a pair of Pydantic models: one for the request body, one for the response. FastAPI parses the incoming JSON into the request model and rejects anything that doesn't fit with a 422 — before a line of your handler runs.

from pydantic import BaseModel, Field


class ChatRequest(BaseModel):
    message: str = Field(min_length=1, max_length=2000)
    temperature: float = Field(default=0.7, ge=0.0, le=2.0)


class ChatResponse(BaseModel):
    reply: str
    model: str
ChatRequest says: "the body must have a non-empty message of at most 2000 characters, and an optional temperature between 0 and 2." A 3000-character message or a missing message is now a clean, automatic 422 with a precise error pointing at the bad field — you never wrote that check yourself. ChatResponse documents and validates what you send back.

This is the same idea as the structured-output schemas from Lecture 2, applied to the edge of your service instead of to the model's output.

Seeing the 422 before FastAPI
You don't need a running server to see what FastAPI does on a bad body. Pydantic is installed in the sandbox, so you can validate the exact same model by hand. A good body parses; a bad one raises a ValidationError, and e.errors() is a list of precise, per-field problems:

from pydantic import BaseModel, Field, ValidationError


class ChatRequest(BaseModel):
    message: str = Field(min_length=1)
    temperature: float = Field(0.7, ge=0, le=2)


good = ChatRequest.model_validate({"message": "Hello!", "temperature": 0.7})
print("OK  ->", good)

try:
    ChatRequest.model_validate({"message": "", "temperature": 9})
except ValidationError as e:
    print("422 ->", e.errors())
The bad request prints two errors — one for the empty message (type: "string_too_short") and one for the out-of-range temperature (type: "less_than_equal"). FastAPI turns exactly this ValidationError into the 422 response body the client receives. So the JSON your frontend sees on a 422 looks like this — a detail field holding a list of per-field errors:

{"detail": [
  {"loc": ["body", "message"], "msg": "String should have at least 1 character",
   "type": "string_too_short"},
  {"loc": ["body", "temperature"], "msg": "Input should be less than or equal to 2",
   "type": "less_than_equal"}
]}
(The real response also carries an input field per error showing the offending value; trimmed here for brevity.)

Every error you raise yourself with HTTPException(status_code, detail="...") uses the same detail field, but with a plain string instead of a list:

{"detail": "upstream error"}
Knowing this shape matters: in Lecture 4 the frontend will read detail to decide what to show the user — a per-field form error for a list, a toast for a string.

4. The FastAPI handler
With the schemas in place, the handler is small. It declares the verb and path, takes the typed body, and returns the typed response:

from fastapi import APIRouter, Depends, HTTPException
# User, current_user come from this app's security layer (kmitl.security.deps)

router = APIRouter(prefix="/api")
MODEL = "gemma-4-E4B-it"


@router.post("/chat", response_model=ChatResponse)
async def chat(req: ChatRequest, user: User = Depends(current_user)) -> ChatResponse:
    if not await rate_limiter.allow(user.id):
        raise HTTPException(status_code=429, detail="Too many requests")
    reply = await chat_service.complete(req.message, req.temperature)
    return ChatResponse(reply=reply, model=MODEL)
A few things to notice:

@router.post("/chat", ...) binds the verb and path in one place.
req: ChatRequest is all it takes for FastAPI to parse and validate the JSON body. response_model=ChatResponse validates and documents the output.
Depends(current_user) pulls the logged-in user from the session cookie, so we know who is calling — needed for per-user rate limiting and logging.
The handler raises HTTPException to produce error responses; FastAPI turns them into the right status code and JSON.
This snippet is pseudo-real, not copy-paste runnable. User and current_user come from this app's security layer (kmitl.security.deps.current_user); chat_service and rate_limiter are objects you'd inject (construct once at startup and pass in). The shape is exactly what the real code looks like, but it leans on wiring defined elsewhere — so to experiment with the logic, use the sandbox-safe simulation in §6.
The handler does almost no work itself. The actual logic — building the message list, calling the model — lives in chat_service. That separation is the next point. (A real chat endpoint usually goes one step further and streams tokens back as they're generated rather than returning one final JSON blob — that's Lecture 4.)

5. House style: router → service → repository
This course's backend (and the code you write for the homeworks) follows a three-layer shape. Keeping to it makes code easy to test and to reason about:

router — HTTP only. Path, verb, status codes, auth dependency, and mapping errors to responses. No business logic.
service — the business logic. Build the messages, call the LLM client, enforce the rate limit, log the call. This is the "fat middle."
repository — pure data access (the database). One class per aggregate, no business logic.
A first /api/chat may not need a repository at all. But the moment you want to persist conversation history — so a returning user sees their past turns — that read/write belongs in a repository, the service orchestrates it, and the router still just speaks HTTP. The payoff: you can unit-test the service (the interesting part) by calling a plain async function, with no HTTP server and no FastAPI in the test.

# service layer — testable without HTTP
from openai import AsyncOpenAI

class ChatService:
    def __init__(self, client: AsyncOpenAI):
        self._client = client

    async def complete(self, message: str, temperature: float) -> str:
        resp = await self._client.chat.completions.create(
            model="gemma-4-E4B-it",
            temperature=temperature,
            messages=[
                {"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful, terse assistant."},
                {"role": "user", "content": message},
            ],
        )
        return resp.choices[0].message.content
Why AsyncOpenAI and await? The handler is async, so it runs on the event loop that serves every request. A blocking call there — like the sync OpenAI client, which waits seconds for the model — would freeze all other requests until it returns. Use the async client and await it (or wrap a sync call in asyncio.to_thread) so the loop stays free while the model thinks. This is what "async everywhere" buys you.
The repository layer, with an ORM
The service above has no database yet. The moment you persist conversation history, that read/write belongs in the repository — pure data access, no business logic. The repository is usually built on an ORM (Object-Relational Mapper), which turns table rows into typed Python objects and lets you compose queries instead of hand-writing SQL strings. With SQLAlchemy 2.x that looks like:

# data layer — display-only (SQLAlchemy is not in the in-browser sandbox)
from datetime import datetime

from sqlalchemy import String, func, select
from sqlalchemy.orm import DeclarativeBase, Mapped, Session, mapped_column


class Base(DeclarativeBase):
    pass


class Conversation(Base):                 # one row = one stored chat turn
    __tablename__ = "conversations"

    id: Mapped[int] = mapped_column(primary_key=True)
    user_id: Mapped[str] = mapped_column(String(64), index=True)
    message: Mapped[str]
    reply: Mapped[str]
    created_at: Mapped[datetime] = mapped_column(server_default=func.now())


class ConversationRepository:             # pure data access — NO business logic
    def __init__(self, session: Session):
        self._session = session

    def add(self, user_id: str, message: str, reply: str) -> Conversation:
        turn = Conversation(user_id=user_id, message=message, reply=reply)
        self._session.add(turn)
        self._session.flush()
        return turn

    def recent(self, user_id: str, limit: int = 5) -> list[Conversation]:
        stmt = (
            select(Conversation)
            .where(Conversation.user_id == user_id)
            .order_by(Conversation.id.desc())
            .limit(limit)
        )
        return list(self._session.scalars(stmt))
Three things to read off this:

*The repository is the only code that touches the database.* The service calls repo.add(...) / repo.recent(...); the router never sees a query. One class per aggregate (here, Conversation), each method a single read or write.
The ORM gives you typed rows and composable queries. Conversation columns are Mapped[...], so turn.reply is a real typed attribute, and select(...) builds a query you can .where(...).order_by(...).limit(...) — no SQL strings.
The table this maps to is exactly what a migration creates. The conversations table with these columns is what Alembic's op.create_table(...) owns — the migration section later in this lecture creates this very table.
Runnable in the reference app: examples/orm_repository.py (this model + repository over in-memory SQLite) and examples/layering.py (the same repository wired under a service and router, with the service unit-tested by a plain call).

6. Simulating the handler (sandbox-safe)
You can't run FastAPI inside the in-browser sandbox, but you can see the whole shape of the handler with a plain function: take a request dict, validate it, call the chat API, and return a response dict. This is exactly what the FastAPI handler does, minus the framework plumbing.

from openai import OpenAI

client = OpenAI()
MODEL = "gemma-4-E4B-it"


def chat_endpoint(request: dict) -> dict:
    # 1. Validate (Pydantic does this for you in real FastAPI).
    msg = request.get("message")
    if not isinstance(msg, str) or not msg.strip():
        return {"status": 422, "error": "message is required"}
    if len(msg) > 2000:
        return {"status": 422, "error": "message too long"}

    # 2. Call the model — the service's job. Hide failures behind 502.
    try:
        resp = client.chat.completions.create(
            model=MODEL,
            temperature=request.get("temperature", 0.7),
            messages=[
                {"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful, terse assistant."},
                {"role": "user", "content": msg},
            ],
        )
    except Exception as e:
        return {"status": 502, "error": f"upstream model error: {e}"}

    # 3. Shape the response (ChatResponse).
    return {"status": 200, "reply": resp.choices[0].message.content, "model": MODEL}


print(chat_endpoint({"message": "Why hide the API key on the server?"}))
print(chat_endpoint({"message": "   "}))  # -> 422
Run it: the good request returns a 200 with a reply; the empty one returns a 422. Map status to a real HTTP status and request: dict to a ChatRequest, and you have the FastAPI handler from §4.

7. Errors are part of the API
Failures are not exceptional — they are a normal part of the contract. Two rules:

Map each failure to a status code the client can act on.
Log the detail server-side; return a safe, generic message to the client. Never leak a stack trace or — worst of all — the API key.
if not req.message.strip():
    raise HTTPException(status_code=422, detail="message is required")
try:
    reply = await call_model(req)
except httpx.TimeoutException:
    raise HTTPException(status_code=504, detail="model timed out")
except Exception:
    log.exception("chat failed")                       # full detail in YOUR logs
    raise HTTPException(status_code=502, detail="upstream error")  # vague to client
The client sees "upstream error"; you see the full traceback in your logs. That asymmetry is deliberate — it's good security and good debugging.

8. A simple token-bucket rate limit
A token bucket is the standard way to cap request rate. Each user has a bucket that holds up to cap tokens and refills at a steady rate per second. Every request spends one token; if the bucket is empty, you reject with 429.

import time
from dataclasses import dataclass


@dataclass
class Bucket:
    tokens: float
    last: float


def allow(bucket: Bucket, rate: float = 0.5, cap: float = 30) -> bool:
    now = time.monotonic()
    # refill for the elapsed time, capped
    bucket.tokens = min(cap, bucket.tokens + (now - bucket.last) * rate)
    bucket.last = now
    if bucket.tokens >= 1:
        bucket.tokens -= 1
        return True
    return False  # -> 429 Too Many Requests
With rate=0.5 and cap=30 this allows 30 requests per minute per user, with short bursts up to 30. An in-process token bucket keyed by user id like this is the standard approach: it is simple and correct for a single server. If you run multiple replicas, move the buckets to a shared store like Redis so the limit is global rather than per-process.

When you reject with a 429, be kind to the client: also send a Retry-After header saying how many seconds to wait before trying again. With this bucket the wait until the next token is (1 - bucket.tokens) / rate seconds. The frontend can read that header and time its "slow down — try again in N seconds" toast instead of hammering the endpoint:

raise HTTPException(
    status_code=429,
    detail="slow down",
    headers={"Retry-After": str(retry_seconds)},
)
A note on idempotency
A GET can be retried freely — it has no side effects. A POST /api/chat is different: each call spends tokens and costs money, so blindly retrying a request whose response got lost on the network can double-charge you and double-run the model. The standard fix is an idempotency key: the client sends a unique id with the request, the server remembers the result for that id, and a retry with the same key returns the stored result instead of calling the model again. Stripe's write-up below is the canonical treatment.

9. Testing your LLM endpoint
Section 5 promised that the router → service → repository split makes your code testable. Here's the payoff — plus the one twist that LLM code adds.

The twist: the model is non-deterministic. You cannot write assert reply == "The total is 96 baht." — ask the same question twice and the wording changes. So you split the problem in two:

The scaffolding is deterministic — message assembly, validation, error mapping (422/502), the rate limiter, response shaping. This you unit-test normally, with the model mocked at the boundary so no network is involved.
The model's answer quality is non-deterministic — judged separately by an eval harness (Lecture 8), not a unit test.
Unit test: mock the model, test the scaffolding
Because ChatService takes its client by injection (§5), a test can pass a fake client that returns a canned reply — no real call, no network, instant and repeatable:

# test_chat_service.py  (display-only — pytest isn't in the class sandbox)
from types import SimpleNamespace
import pytest


class FakeChatClient:
    """Stand-in for AsyncOpenAI: .chat.completions.create returns a canned reply."""
    def __init__(self, reply):
        self.reply = reply
        self.seen = None
        self.chat = SimpleNamespace(completions=self)

    async def create(self, **kwargs):
        self.seen = kwargs                       # record the call for assertions
        message = SimpleNamespace(content=self.reply)
        return SimpleNamespace(choices=[SimpleNamespace(message=message)])


@pytest.mark.asyncio
async def test_complete_builds_the_right_messages():
    fake = FakeChatClient("Paris is the capital of France.")
    svc = ChatService(fake)
    reply = await svc.complete("Capital of France?", temperature=0)
    # assert on the SCAFFOLDING, not on the model's exact words:
    assert [m["role"] for m in fake.seen["messages"]] == ["system", "user"]
    assert fake.seen["temperature"] == 0         # we passed the knob through
    assert "Paris" in reply                       # a PROPERTY of the output, not ==
Notice the assertions: we check the messages we built and that the reply contains an expected token — never that it equals an exact string. Asserting properties (contains, is-valid-JSON, has-the-required-fields) is how you test non-deterministic output.

End-to-end test: drive the real route, mock only the model
A unit test skips HTTP. An e2e test exercises the whole route — routing, Pydantic validation, status codes, response shape — by calling the app in process with httpx's AsyncClient over an ASGI transport. No live server, no port, no network; the LLM is still mocked via FastAPI's dependency override:

# test_chat_route.py  (display-only)
import httpx
import pytest
from myapp.main import app
from myapp.deps import get_chat_service

app.dependency_overrides[get_chat_service] = lambda: ChatService(FakeChatClient("hi there"))


@pytest.mark.asyncio
async def test_chat_route_happy_path():
    transport = httpx.ASGITransport(app=app)            # in-process, no network
    async with httpx.AsyncClient(transport=transport, base_url="http://t") as ac:
        r = await ac.post("/api/chat", json={"message": "hello"})
    assert r.status_code == 200
    assert r.json()["reply"] == "hi there"              # the mock makes this exact


@pytest.mark.asyncio
async def test_chat_route_rejects_empty():
    transport = httpx.ASGITransport(app=app)
    async with httpx.AsyncClient(transport=transport, base_url="http://t") as ac:
        r = await ac.post("/api/chat", json={"message": "   "})
    assert r.status_code == 422                          # Pydantic/handler rejects it
This is exactly how the reference app is tested — see its tests/test_e2e.py: an in-process httpx.AsyncClient over an ASGITransport pointed at the FastAPI app, with the LLM swapped for a fake so the route runs fast and deterministically. No live server, no port, no network.

Three tools, three questions
Test	Question it answers	Model
Unit	Does my scaffolding logic work? (messages, validation, errors)	mocked
e2e	Does the whole route wire up? (status codes, response shape)	mocked
Eval (L8)	Is the model's answer actually good?	real
Don't reach for an eval when a unit test fits, and don't unit-test the model's prose. Mock the model in unit/e2e tests; save the real model for evals.

Runnable: stub the model, assert the scaffolding
pytest isn't in the sandbox, but the idea runs in plain Python — a stub instead of the model, and assert instead of a test framework:

def build_messages(system, user):            # the deterministic scaffolding
    return [{"role": "system", "content": system},
            {"role": "user", "content": user}]

def fake_model(messages):                     # stub: no network, fixed reply
    return "The total is 96 baht."

def handle(system, user):
    msgs = build_messages(system, user)
    reply = fake_model(msgs)                   # in prod: client.chat.completions.create(...)
    return {"messages": msgs, "reply": reply}

out = handle("You are terse.", "Sum 90 and 6 in baht.")
# assert PROPERTIES, never the model's exact words:
assert [m["role"] for m in out["messages"]] == ["system", "user"]
assert "96" in out["reply"]
print("all assertions passed")
Swap fake_model for a real client.chat.completions.create(...) and the scaffolding is unchanged — which is the whole point of mocking at the boundary.

Key terms
REST — an architectural style: resources at URLs, manipulated with HTTP verbs.
Verb / method — GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE; says what to do.
Status code — the numeric result: 2xx ok, 4xx client error, 5xx server error.
Pydantic model / schema — a typed class that validates and documents a request or response body.
Handler / router — the function bound to a verb + path; the HTTP edge.
Service layer — where business logic lives, independent of HTTP.
Rate limit / token bucket — capping request rate per user to control cost and abuse; the bucket refills over time and each call spends a token.
Unit test — exercises one function/service in isolation with the model mocked; asserts on the deterministic scaffolding (messages, validation, errors).
End-to-end (e2e) test — drives the whole HTTP route in-process (httpx AsyncClient), model still mocked; checks status codes and response shape.
Mock / stub — a fake stand-in for the model client that returns a canned reply, so tests run fast, offline, and repeatably.
Eval vs. test — an eval judges the real model's answer quality (Lecture 8); a test checks your deterministic code with the model mocked.
