Database migrations with Alembic
The moment your backend has a database — conversation history (§5), users, or a vector store for retrieval (Lecture 6) — its schema has to change over time: you add a column, a table, an index. A quick demo can get away with a CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS run once at startup, but that doesn't evolve a table that already holds data, and it leaves you hand-editing production tables through a psql prompt — unreviewed, unordered, and impossible to undo cleanly.

A migration is the disciplined alternative. Each schema change is a small, versioned, ordered, reversible script. Alembic (the migration tool for SQLAlchemy) keeps these scripts in alembic/versions/, each one chained to the one before it, and records in the database which revisions have already run. So every environment — your laptop, staging, production — converges to the same schema by replaying the same ordered list.

The workflow is three commands. You generate a revision, fill in its two functions, then apply it:

alembic revision -m "add conversations table"   # create an empty revision
alembic upgrade head                             # apply everything up to latest
alembic downgrade -1                             # roll back the last one
upgrade() makes the change; downgrade() undoes it, so a bad migration can be reversed instead of debugged in production:

def upgrade() -> None:
    op.create_table(
        "conversations",
        sa.Column("id", sa.Integer, primary_key=True),
        sa.Column("user_id", sa.Integer, sa.ForeignKey("users.id")),
        sa.Column("message", sa.Text, nullable=False),
    )

def downgrade() -> None:
    op.drop_table("conversations")
This isn't hypothetical — the reference app runs on it. Its migrations live in alembic/versions/, and the workflow is the same one any real backend uses: a schema change is a reviewed revision, and alembic upgrade head brings every environment — your laptop, staging, production — to the same schema in lockstep with the code that expects it.

Concretely, the reference app uses this for its Postgres + pgvector path. Its alembic/versions/0001_create_chunks.py creates the chunks table with a vector(1024) column for embeddings. To run it, docker compose up -d brings up Postgres + pgvector (on host port 55432, deliberately not the default 5432 so it can't clash with a local Postgres), then uv sync --group pg, set DATABASE_URL, and alembic upgrade head. Its app/vectorstore.py switches between SQLite and Postgres purely by whether DATABASE_URL is set — the same code, two backends, one migrated schema.

